1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:01,163
Tracera.

2
00:00:01,163 --> 00:00:02,938
From symptom to certainty.

3
00:00:02,938 --> 00:00:10,763
Show it a bug, and it investigates the code,
proposes a fix, verifies that fix in a
sandbox, and shows you what changed.

4
00:00:11,212 --> 00:00:13,562
A bug report is only a symptom.

5
00:00:13,562 --> 00:00:19,174
Getting to a fix you can trust means reading
code, reproducing the failure, and checking
the patch.

6
00:00:19,174 --> 00:00:24,949
Tracera does that legwork, and it keeps one
rule: it never claims more than it observed.

7
00:00:24,949 --> 00:00:33,762
Nothing is called verified, fixed, or
confirmed unless the stored record says so,
and every model result is labelled live or
mock.

8
00:00:34,218 --> 00:00:36,830
You will see Tracera in two environments.

9
00:00:36,831 --> 00:00:41,968
A hosted website, where you can look around,
ask questions, and run analysis.

10
00:00:41,968 --> 00:00:53,656
And your own laptop, where your code, your
database, and the Docker sandbox stay on
your machine, and where the full workflow
runs: sandboxed reproduction, verification,
and applying a patch.

11
00:00:53,656 --> 00:00:58,893
The laptop needs the internet only for model
calls and the optional Google Meet import.


12
00:00:59,833 --> 00:01:04,273
This table shows what works in the hosted
demo, and what works in the local app.

13
00:01:04,573 --> 00:01:11,557
Bug analysis, screenshot understanding and
recording analysis work in both, because
none of them needs Docker.

14
00:01:11,857 --> 00:01:18,769
The hosted demo has no Docker, so sandbox
verification, apply, commit and push are
unavailable there.

15
00:01:19,069 --> 00:01:23,485
Locally they work, once Docker is configured
and an owner is signed in.

16
00:01:23,785 --> 00:01:27,625
Google Meet import needs configuration in
both places.


17
00:01:28,525 --> 00:01:33,565
System context: who uses Tracera, and which
outside systems it depends on.

18
00:01:33,865 --> 00:01:41,257
A reviewer uses the web app, a bug reporter
uses the Chrome extension, and a coding
agent uses the MCP server.

19
00:01:41,557 --> 00:01:47,797
All three call one Tracera server over
HTTPS, and its state lives in PostgreSQL.

20
00:01:48,097 --> 00:01:57,337
Dashed boxes are outside systems: the model
API, Google, Supabase Auth, the npm
registry, Docker and your own git remote.

21
00:01:57,637 --> 00:02:00,853
Docker, installs and git push are local
only.


22
00:02:01,753 --> 00:02:05,041
Containers: what runs, and what stores
state.

23
00:02:05,341 --> 00:02:14,293
The API process serves the web app, the
extension and the MCP server, and enqueues
jobs. It never calls Docker or a model.

24
00:02:14,593 --> 00:02:22,273
The worker process runs every model call,
through the inference gateway, and every
sandbox run, through the Docker executor.

25
00:02:22,573 --> 00:02:30,805
The two processes never call each other.
They share PostgreSQL, for rows and the job
queue, and a data directory for files.


26
00:02:31,705 --> 00:02:37,009
One TypeScript monorepo holds three clients,
one server and shared schemas.

27
00:02:37,309 --> 00:02:45,229
The web app, the extension and the MCP
server import only the contracts package,
and call the server over HTTP.

28
00:02:45,529 --> 00:02:51,697
In the server, the API and the worker call
the modules, which are flat files grouped by
area.

29
00:02:51,997 --> 00:02:54,853
Every model call goes through the inference
folder.

30
00:02:55,153 --> 00:03:01,153
Repository code runs only through the
sandbox folder, which only the worker
process reaches.

31
00:03:01,453 --> 00:03:06,109
Neither folder imports HTTP, database or
module code.


32
00:03:07,059 --> 00:03:10,884
This is the hosted website, running on
free-plan services.

33
00:03:10,884 --> 00:03:15,484
It may be asleep when you arrive, and the
first request can take up to a minute.

34
00:03:15,484 --> 00:03:19,821
The sign-in page offers Continue with
Google, which I am not using here.

35
00:03:19,821 --> 00:03:22,834
I sign in as the public judge account from
the README.

36
00:03:23,297 --> 00:03:26,822
The header says Hosted demo, with live Meta
inference.

37
00:03:26,822 --> 00:03:33,797
Two repositories are registered and indexed:
shop-ui, and bench-shop, a synthetic
benchmark fixture.

38
00:03:33,797 --> 00:03:37,672
Coverage and freshness are reported the way
the API sees them.

39
00:03:38,143 --> 00:03:40,868
The code graph works here as it does on a
laptop.

40
00:03:40,868 --> 00:03:44,880
I search for a symbol, open it, and trace
its callers upstream.

41
00:03:45,333 --> 00:03:51,845
Questions are answered by a live model over
excerpts from that graph, and each claim
cites the code it relies on.

42
00:03:51,845 --> 00:03:53,221
The label says live.

43
00:03:53,675 --> 00:03:59,388
Change impact lists what a change to a
symbol can reach: code, tests, and files.

44
00:03:59,857 --> 00:04:02,894
On the Meetings page I upload a short
transcript.

45
00:04:02,894 --> 00:04:11,032
A live model proposes bug candidates, each
with quotes from the transcript, and nothing
becomes a bug report until a person confirms
it.

46
00:04:11,487 --> 00:04:15,487
An investigation on the hosted site stops
before the sandbox.

47
00:04:15,487 --> 00:04:22,987
I describe the shipping bug, attach the cart
screenshot, and choose analysis only,
because this environment has no Docker.

48
00:04:23,453 --> 00:04:25,203
The worker runs the stages.

49
00:04:25,203 --> 00:04:31,091
The sandbox stages are recorded as blocked,
not faked, and the reproduction section says
so.

50
00:04:31,555 --> 00:04:39,793
The patch review page shows the proposed
one-line diff, and a notice: no sandbox in
this environment, so the fix was not run
here.

51
00:04:39,792 --> 00:04:45,255
The recorded verification outcome is
blocked, with the reason analysis only
requested.

52
00:04:45,255 --> 00:04:46,830
Nothing is called verified.

53
00:04:46,830 --> 00:04:54,367
Applying, committing, and pushing are
laptop-only features; on the hosted site an
approved patch can only be downloaded.


54
00:04:55,325 --> 00:04:58,661
Three ways in, six checks on every request.

55
00:04:58,961 --> 00:05:02,081
A password or Google sign-in creates a
session.

56
00:05:02,381 --> 00:05:09,749
Scripts, the extension and MCP use a
personal access token; secrets are stored
only as hashes.

57
00:05:10,049 --> 00:05:16,505
A request is authenticated, checked for CSRF
if it uses a cookie, then passed to a route
guard.

58
00:05:16,805 --> 00:05:24,485
Queries are scoped to the caller's
workspace, so another workspace's ID answers
not found; limits and audit follow.

59
00:05:24,785 --> 00:05:29,249
A token can never approve a patch, apply,
commit or push.


60
00:05:30,149 --> 00:05:33,437
The token that returns to the browser is
never trusted.

61
00:05:33,737 --> 00:05:39,977
The sign-in page links to Supabase, which
sends the browser to Google and back with an
access token.

62
00:05:40,277 --> 00:05:43,853
The callback page sends only that token to
the API.

63
00:05:44,153 --> 00:05:52,049
The API asks Supabase who owns the token,
and accepts only a Google identity whose
email Google vouches for.

64
00:05:52,349 --> 00:05:58,445
It finds the user by email or creates a new
member, never an owner, and starts a cookie
session.


65
00:05:59,345 --> 00:06:05,249
Only an owner registers a repository, and an
existing snapshot for a commit is reused.

66
00:06:05,549 --> 00:06:13,565
A worker clones a mirror and parses
JavaScript, TypeScript and SQL as text,
running nothing from the repository.

67
00:06:13,865 --> 00:06:19,697
An edge exists only when its target
resolves; otherwise it is counted as
unresolved.

68
00:06:19,997 --> 00:06:26,525
The graph has twenty-seven node kinds and
twenty-two edge kinds, and every edge
records its certainty.

69
00:06:26,825 --> 00:06:34,865
Search, tracing, impact and cited questions
read one snapshot, for the web app, MCP
tools and patch impact.


70
00:06:35,815 --> 00:06:38,915
Back on the laptop stack, with the
repositories.

71
00:06:38,915 --> 00:06:42,190
Registering a repository is an owner-only
action.

72
00:06:42,645 --> 00:06:45,682
I register the Shop UI demo as a local path.

73
00:06:45,682 --> 00:06:51,757
The form stores the install and test
commands for later sandbox runs, and they
never run on the host.

74
00:06:51,757 --> 00:06:58,708
The start command and app port that browser
runs need are set through the API, because
the form does not expose them yet.

75
00:06:59,171 --> 00:07:03,134
Indexing runs in the background worker, and
it only parses text.

76
00:07:03,134 --> 00:07:05,946
Nothing from the repository is executed.

77
00:07:05,946 --> 00:07:11,209
The snapshot reports its own coverage and
freshness, including what it could not
resolve.

78
00:07:11,665 --> 00:07:16,340
Search finds symbols, files, routes, tables,
and text.

79
00:07:16,791 --> 00:07:23,053
A node shows its source excerpt, marked as
observed by the parser, and its direct
relationships.

80
00:07:23,525 --> 00:07:29,037
Tracing follows calls upstream or
downstream, and it lists the gaps it could
not bridge.

81
00:07:29,491 --> 00:07:32,953
History and ownership come from git, not
from a model.

82
00:07:33,417 --> 00:07:40,679
Change impact ranks the code, tests, and
files that a change here can reach, with the
chain of edges that connects them.

83
00:07:41,135 --> 00:07:43,885
Questions about the codebase get cited
answers.

84
00:07:43,885 --> 00:07:47,910
I ask where the shipping charge is decided,
and how the cart page shows it.

85
00:07:47,910 --> 00:07:51,135
A live model reads excerpts that the graph
retrieved.

86
00:07:51,589 --> 00:07:56,564
Every claim cites an excerpt, and code
checks each citation against what was sent.

87
00:07:56,564 --> 00:08:01,627
A claim whose citation does not hold is
shown as unsupported instead of being
hidden.

88
00:08:01,627 --> 00:08:05,064
The label says live: this was a real Muse
Spark call.

89
00:08:05,523 --> 00:08:08,285
The Impact tab also analyses a patch.

90
00:08:08,285 --> 00:08:10,460
I paste the diff Tracera proposed.

91
00:08:10,460 --> 00:08:15,836
It is applied to a Tracera-owned copy of the
base, and the working tree is never touched.

92
00:08:16,289 --> 00:08:25,027
The analysis runs as a job, and reports its
own status and limits: what the diff
changes, which tests cover it, and what it
can reach.


93
00:08:25,965 --> 00:08:29,949
Connecting starts on the Repositories page,
with a signed state.

94
00:08:30,249 --> 00:08:34,305
The owner installs the App on GitHub, which
calls back to Tracera.

95
00:08:34,605 --> 00:08:39,789
The state is accepted only for the same
owner, and the OAuth code proves access.

96
00:08:40,089 --> 00:08:43,305
The installation is then bound to the
workspace.

97
00:08:43,605 --> 00:08:48,141
Repositories are connected one by one;
nothing is indexed by default.

98
00:08:48,441 --> 00:08:53,865
A connection, a webhook, a reconciliation or
the Sync button queues one job.

99
00:08:54,165 --> 00:08:58,533
The worker fetches with a short-lived token
that is held only in memory.

100
00:08:58,833 --> 00:09:02,361
The exact commit it fetched is the one that
gets indexed.


101
00:09:03,311 --> 00:09:10,623
First, the real repository:
lumen-goods-demo, public on GitHub, opened
in this browser while logged out.

102
00:09:10,623 --> 00:09:11,974
It has two commits.

103
00:09:11,974 --> 00:09:17,849
The latest, 6486792, is a refactor of the
free shipping check.

104
00:09:17,849 --> 00:09:19,624
Tracera has not seen it yet.

105
00:09:20,077 --> 00:09:23,527
In Tracera, the Repositories page has a
GitHub panel.

106
00:09:23,527 --> 00:09:29,027
It shows the connected account, vishavmehra,
with access to all repositories.

107
00:09:29,027 --> 00:09:33,839
The status call also says this GitHub App is
not subscribed to the push event.

108
00:09:33,839 --> 00:09:40,127
That is expected: webhooks are optional
here, and the worker reconciles every five
minutes anyway.

109
00:09:40,587 --> 00:09:47,225
GitHub's own install consent page is not
recorded here: that click was done earlier,
in a logged-in browser.

110
00:09:47,225 --> 00:09:49,762
This is step two of the sync diagram.

111
00:09:49,762 --> 00:09:58,012
Tracera accepts the signed install state
only for the same signed-in owner, and the
OAuth code proves the installer can reach
the installation.

112
00:09:58,481 --> 00:10:05,993
Above the list are two commands: install,
npm ci --ignore-scripts, and test, npm test.

113
00:10:05,993 --> 00:10:11,269
They are stored with the repository for
later sandbox runs, and they never run on
the host.

114
00:10:11,719 --> 00:10:15,506
Below them is every repository this
installation can reach.

115
00:10:15,506 --> 00:10:20,769
I installed the App for all of mine, so the
list is long, and private names are blurred.

116
00:10:20,769 --> 00:10:23,044
Only lumen-goods-demo matters here.

117
00:10:23,493 --> 00:10:26,443
Connect is an explicit, owner-only action.

118
00:10:26,443 --> 00:10:37,543
Tracera takes one read-only installation
token for this repository, keeps it in
memory, and hands it to git through its
environment, never in a URL, a log, or a
file.

119
00:10:37,543 --> 00:10:44,631
The fetch goes into a bare cache in the data
directory, under sources, named by the
repository id, dot git.

120
00:10:45,083 --> 00:10:51,221
The sync line appears: indexed 6486792, with
the time of the sync.

121
00:10:51,221 --> 00:10:54,570
The exact fetched commit is the one that was
indexed.

122
00:10:54,570 --> 00:10:56,733
Zero pending, zero failed.

123
00:10:57,193 --> 00:10:58,530
Sync fetches again.

124
00:10:58,530 --> 00:11:04,281
It asks GitHub for the branch head, and if
that commit is already indexed there is
nothing new to do.

125
00:11:04,281 --> 00:11:07,793
Here it is up to date: the same commit stays
indexed.

126
00:11:07,793 --> 00:11:10,306
A push webhook would trigger the same sync.


127
00:11:11,311 --> 00:11:13,373
This is a tour of the rest of the app.

128
00:11:13,373 --> 00:11:18,848
The sidebar has three sections:
Repositories, Investigations, and Meetings.

129
00:11:18,849 --> 00:11:27,036
In the top bar, the Local chip says this
workspace runs on the laptop, and the
inference label says live, meaning a real
model call.

130
00:11:27,036 --> 00:11:29,473
A mock run would be labelled mock instead.

131
00:11:29,925 --> 00:11:37,013
Investigations lists every bug
investigation, with its status, the commit
it ran against, and when it started.

132
00:11:37,013 --> 00:11:39,925
Statuses and outcomes are the stored values.

133
00:11:39,925 --> 00:11:43,013
This one is completed, and its patch is
approved.

134
00:11:43,475 --> 00:11:55,850
An investigation opens with its stages along
the top, each showing the stored state:
reported, twenty-eight evidence items,
reproduced, confirmed, approved, verified,
and report written.

135
00:11:55,850 --> 00:12:00,650
Below them are the repository, the commit,
the mode, and the inference label.

136
00:12:01,105 --> 00:12:08,217
The bug report panel holds the normalized
report: the description, the expected
behavior, and the actual behavior.

137
00:12:08,217 --> 00:12:09,730
This one came from a meeting.

138
00:12:10,191 --> 00:12:12,316
Findings lists the hypotheses.

139
00:12:12,316 --> 00:12:20,379
This one is marked confirmed by
verification, and it shows its confidence,
where in the code it applies, and the
citations that support it.

140
00:12:20,837 --> 00:12:30,812
Evidence is grouped by how it was obtained:
observed facts recorded by a tool, claims
reported in the bug report, and model
observations, which are labelled inferred.

141
00:12:30,812 --> 00:12:37,187
Each item has a handle, such as CODE-4, and
items that a hypothesis relies on are marked
cited.

142
00:12:37,651 --> 00:12:45,076
Reproduction lists every sandbox run with
its command, exit code, and duration, before
and after the patch.

143
00:12:45,076 --> 00:12:47,901
They ran in the sandbox, never on the host.

144
00:12:48,369 --> 00:12:50,981
Patches lists each attempt with its
decision.

145
00:12:50,982 --> 00:12:56,269
Attempt one is approved, and its check reads
verified against the original reproduction.

146
00:12:56,269 --> 00:13:00,269
Workflow lists the pipeline stages with
their status and duration.

147
00:13:00,269 --> 00:13:04,482
The page says these are stage records as
stored, not a full event log.

148
00:13:04,943 --> 00:13:16,018
The resolution report collects the reported
issue, the reproduction, the root cause, the
proposed change, the verification checks and
environment, the decisions, and the next
step.

149
00:13:16,018 --> 00:13:18,018
It can be downloaded as Markdown.

150
00:13:18,469 --> 00:13:22,656
Meetings turns what was said into bug
candidates that cite the transcript.

151
00:13:22,656 --> 00:13:25,994
Nothing becomes a bug report until a person
confirms it.

152
00:13:25,994 --> 00:13:32,681
The page has the Google Meet connection, a
transcript upload with a consent check, and
the list of imported meetings.

153
00:13:33,147 --> 00:13:37,959
A meeting page shows its source, the
transcript segments, and the candidates.

154
00:13:37,959 --> 00:13:43,159
A model proposed them, and code checked that
every quote appears in the segment it cites.

155
00:13:43,159 --> 00:13:49,347
They stay proposed, not confirmed, until a
person confirms, edits, or dismisses them.

156
00:13:49,347 --> 00:13:51,359
Names are masked in this recording.

157
00:13:51,809 --> 00:13:56,034
The account menu shows who is signed in, and
Sign out ends the session.

158
00:13:56,503 --> 00:14:04,203
At phone width, 375 pixels, the sidebar
becomes a bottom tab bar, and the pages
stack into one column.


159
00:14:05,128 --> 00:14:09,784
Patch impact shows what a patch can reach
from static graphs; nothing is run.

160
00:14:10,084 --> 00:14:13,348
The input is two revisions, or a pasted
diff.

161
00:14:13,648 --> 00:14:19,096
It compares the revisions, graphs both
sides, and maps changed lines to symbols.

162
00:14:19,396 --> 00:14:26,260
It follows the graph outward from changed
symbols and links tests, reporting none
identified, never untested.

163
00:14:26,560 --> 00:14:31,000
Severity and confidence are rated
separately, by documented rules.

164
00:14:31,300 --> 00:14:35,524
The result appears on the Impact page and
with each proposed patch.


165
00:14:36,424 --> 00:14:42,304
One bug, end to end. Orange means a person
decides; grey needs the Docker sandbox.

166
00:14:42,604 --> 00:14:49,420
A report arrives, code searches the
repository, and the sandbox runs a
reproduction before any patch.

167
00:14:49,720 --> 00:14:55,576
Models propose causes and patches, and code
checks every citation and the patch policy.

168
00:14:55,876 --> 00:15:02,140
The sandbox applies the exact diff the
reviewer sees, and a template writes the
report from stored data.

169
00:15:02,440 --> 00:15:09,232
A person reviews, and approval changes no
files. Applying, committing and pushing are
separate requests.

170
00:15:09,532 --> 00:15:12,820
Models never act, and nothing runs on the
host.


171
00:15:13,720 --> 00:15:19,792
Ten stages run as separate queued jobs, and
the status changes only along the drawn
arrows.

172
00:15:20,092 --> 00:15:23,404
Each box gives a timeout and whether failure
is fatal.

173
00:15:23,704 --> 00:15:29,056
Dashed arrows allow another patch attempt,
or a jump to the report when nothing can
continue.

174
00:15:29,356 --> 00:15:34,420
A starting job makes the status running, and
a proposed patch makes it awaiting approval.

175
00:15:34,720 --> 00:15:39,736
Approved or rejected completes it, and
changes requested send it back to queued.

176
00:15:40,036 --> 00:15:45,316
A fatal failure or three failed attempts
ends as failed, and retry requeues it.


177
00:15:46,216 --> 00:15:49,768
Four kinds of claim, weakest to strongest
evidence.

178
00:15:50,068 --> 00:15:54,868
Visible is what a model reads from an image;
inferred is what it guesses.

179
00:15:55,168 --> 00:16:00,952
Confirmed by repository inspection is
static: tools recorded it from code and
history.

180
00:16:01,252 --> 00:16:06,844
Verified by execution means sandbox runs,
and the stored outcome must say so.

181
00:16:07,144 --> 00:16:13,024
Models propose, code sets hypothesis states,
and the UI labels mock or live.


182
00:16:13,924 --> 00:16:18,268
Only one function writes the outcome, and
the first matching rule wins.

183
00:16:18,568 --> 00:16:22,888
Its input is one record per check, with a
kind and a status.

184
00:16:23,188 --> 00:16:27,052
No checks gives not attempted, and any
failed check gives failed.

185
00:16:27,352 --> 00:16:31,408
Verified needs a passed reproduction and
every check passed.

186
00:16:31,708 --> 00:16:36,220
Passing checks without a passed reproduction
give only partially verified.

187
00:16:36,520 --> 00:16:41,752
Inconclusive checks give inconclusive, and
checks that could not run give blocked.

188
00:16:42,052 --> 00:16:48,436
A passing test suite alone never gives
verified, and apply, commit and push must
echo the outcome.


189
00:16:49,386 --> 00:16:56,524
This is the laptop environment: Tracera
running on this machine, with live Meta
models and the Docker sandbox.

190
00:16:56,524 --> 00:16:59,111
I sign in with an email and a password.

191
00:16:59,576 --> 00:17:06,426
Two repositories are registered and indexed:
shop-ui, a small shop with a cart page, and
bench-shop.

192
00:17:06,886 --> 00:17:08,324
Now the Meetings page.

193
00:17:08,324 --> 00:17:14,386
Google Meet is connected with read-only
access, granted earlier through Google's own
consent screen.

194
00:17:14,386 --> 00:17:20,636
Tracera receives the transcript text only:
no audio, no video, no screen shares.

195
00:17:21,084 --> 00:17:28,959
To import, I choose a default repository,
confirm that I am authorized to share the
transcript, and ask Google for recent
meetings.

196
00:17:29,426 --> 00:17:34,076
Google lists a real meeting from today, with
two generated transcripts.

197
00:17:34,528 --> 00:17:36,028
I import the longer one.


198
00:17:37,250 --> 00:17:40,538
A meeting transcript becomes reviewed bug
reports.

199
00:17:40,838 --> 00:17:48,206
A signed-in person connects Google with a
proof key and a read-only scope; tokens are
sealed and never leave the server.

200
00:17:48,506 --> 00:17:51,650
Importing a transcript needs a consent
attestation.

201
00:17:51,950 --> 00:17:58,646
Numbered segments go to one model call,
which proposes candidates with quotes from
the untrusted transcript.

202
00:17:58,946 --> 00:18:05,810
Code rejects any candidate whose quote is
not verbatim in the segments sent. The model
sets no status.

203
00:18:06,110 --> 00:18:15,230
A person edits, dismisses or confirms each
candidate; confirming creates a bug report
and, unless declined, an investigation.


204
00:18:16,180 --> 00:18:18,967
Tracera parses it into numbered segments.

205
00:18:18,967 --> 00:18:23,030
This one is forty-eight seconds, and the
speaker lists four bugs.

206
00:18:23,030 --> 00:18:26,280
Speaker names from the source are masked in
this recording.

207
00:18:26,730 --> 00:18:32,392
Now a live model reads the transcript and
proposes bug candidates, each with quotes.

208
00:18:32,393 --> 00:18:36,530
This is a real call to Meta's Muse Spark,
and the wait is sped up.

209
00:18:36,530 --> 00:18:37,955
The model proposes.

210
00:18:37,955 --> 00:18:44,055
Code then checks that every quote occurs
verbatim in the segment it cites, and flags
likely duplicates.

211
00:18:44,504 --> 00:19:00,692
Four candidates: the checkout button does
not respond, the price does not update when
the quantity changes, the mobile navigation
overlaps the content, and the cart banner
promises free shipping from fifty dollars,
yet an order of exactly fifty is charged
four ninety-nine.

212
00:19:00,692 --> 00:19:04,929
Each carries the quote that supports it, and
none of them is a bug report yet.

213
00:19:05,398 --> 00:19:07,548
I confirm the free-shipping candidate.

214
00:19:07,548 --> 00:19:10,798
Confirming creates a bug report and nothing
else.

215
00:19:10,798 --> 00:19:14,086
A person still decides whether an
investigation starts.

216
00:19:14,556 --> 00:19:20,318
I start one on shop-ui in full mode, which
means sandboxed reproduction and
verification.

217
00:19:20,786 --> 00:19:23,236
The worker then ran all ten stages.

218
00:19:23,236 --> 00:19:32,386
This recording does not show the wait
itself: from here on, everything on screen
is stored state, and each stage keeps its
status and its duration.

219
00:19:32,848 --> 00:19:42,898
One hypothesis: an off-by-one in the
shipping function, which uses a strict
greater-than, so a subtotal of exactly fifty
dollars falls through to the paid rate.

220
00:19:42,898 --> 00:19:50,385
It cites code excerpts, the commit that
introduced the change, the baseline
reproduction, and the bug report as
submitted.

221
00:19:50,385 --> 00:19:55,698
Its state is confirmed by verification, and
a rule set that state, not the model.

222
00:19:56,166 --> 00:20:00,653
The reproduction section records what the
sandbox observed on the original code.

223
00:20:00,653 --> 00:20:05,753
A real browser followed the steps against
the running app, and the baseline reproduced
the bug.

224
00:20:06,212 --> 00:20:09,624
The proposed patch changes one line in one
file.

225
00:20:10,090 --> 00:20:16,627
The review page shows the exact diff that
was verified: the strict greater-than
becomes greater-than-or-equal.

226
00:20:17,088 --> 00:20:21,013
Static impact comes from the code graph,
with its limits stated.

227
00:20:21,470 --> 00:20:29,095
The recorded outcome is verified: the
reproduction check passed on the patched
code, the unit tests passed, and no check
failed.

228
00:20:29,095 --> 00:20:32,295
Both ran in the Docker sandbox, with no
network.

229
00:20:32,295 --> 00:20:35,145
A rule derived it from what the sandbox
observed.

230
00:20:35,596 --> 00:20:39,646
Runtime evidence shows the same browser
steps before and after.

231
00:20:39,646 --> 00:20:46,659
Before the patch: a subtotal of fifty
dollars, shipping four ninety-nine, and the
free-shipping assertion fails.

232
00:20:46,659 --> 00:20:50,796
After the patch: shipping reads Free, and
the assertions pass.

233
00:20:51,258 --> 00:20:54,745
A person acknowledges the recorded outcome
and approves.

234
00:20:54,746 --> 00:20:56,546
Approval records a decision.

235
00:20:56,546 --> 00:20:58,183
It changes no files.

236
00:20:58,640 --> 00:21:04,115
Applying the patch is a separate action,
with its own authorization and its own
confirmation.

237
00:21:04,115 --> 00:21:10,203
It writes the one-line change into the
working tree of the registered repository,
and it stages nothing.

238
00:21:10,203 --> 00:21:13,290
No commit and no push were run in this
recording.

239
00:21:13,750 --> 00:21:16,075
I start the app from that patched working
tree.

240
00:21:16,075 --> 00:21:20,375
With one lamp the cart is twenty-five
dollars, and shipping is charged.

241
00:21:20,844 --> 00:21:26,019
With two lamps the subtotal is exactly fifty
dollars, and shipping now reads Free.

242
00:21:26,019 --> 00:21:35,031
That is the whole loop on the laptop: from a
Google Meet transcript to a reviewed,
applied change, with every claim tied to a
stored record.


243
00:21:35,997 --> 00:21:41,085
Every model call passes through one gateway,
and nothing else imports a provider.

244
00:21:41,385 --> 00:21:45,009
The registry defines each task's prompt and
schema.

245
00:21:45,309 --> 00:21:51,285
The gateway redacts secrets, prepares
images, hashes the request and checks
budgets.

246
00:21:51,585 --> 00:21:56,097
It then calls the provider, with retries,
and validates the JSON.

247
00:21:56,397 --> 00:21:59,661
Every run records one model executions row.

248
00:21:59,961 --> 00:22:04,809
Providers: Meta, an OpenAI-style endpoint,
or a labelled mock.


249
00:22:05,709 --> 00:22:11,325
Repository code and model-written tests run
only in disposable Docker containers.

250
00:22:11,625 --> 00:22:16,929
Each run gets no network, a read-only root,
dropped capabilities and limits.

251
00:22:17,229 --> 00:22:23,853
Dependencies install behind a proxy allowing
only the registry, then are sealed and
mounted read-only.

252
00:22:24,153 --> 00:22:28,425
The browser reaches only the app and aborts
every other request.

253
00:22:28,725 --> 00:22:33,453
Without Docker, stages are recorded as
blocked; nothing runs on the host.


254
00:22:34,403 --> 00:22:39,503
Repository code is untrusted, so Tracera
never runs it on the host.

255
00:22:39,503 --> 00:22:44,803
Tests and reproductions run only inside
Docker, through one sandbox executor.

256
00:22:44,803 --> 00:22:49,440
What you see is its real output, captured
live on this laptop and replayed here.

257
00:22:49,897 --> 00:22:56,709
A request carries only data: a command, a
workspace, an allowlisted environment, and a
timeout.

258
00:22:56,709 --> 00:22:58,922
The executor validates it first.

259
00:22:58,922 --> 00:23:06,309
A shell, inline code, a host variable, and a
workspace outside the root were each refused
before Docker was called.

260
00:23:06,759 --> 00:23:12,234
Inside the container, the process ran as
user 1000, group 1000.

261
00:23:12,234 --> 00:23:19,021
Writes to work, etc, root, and usr local bin
all failed with a read-only file system
error.

262
00:23:19,021 --> 00:23:23,921
Only tmp accepted a write, and running a
script from tmp was denied.

263
00:23:24,389 --> 00:23:26,527
There is only a loopback interface.

264
00:23:26,527 --> 00:23:31,339
A connection to 1.1.1.1 failed as
unreachable.

265
00:23:31,339 --> 00:23:34,064
DNS and fetch both failed to resolve.

266
00:23:34,064 --> 00:23:40,327
Every capability set reads zero,
no-new-privileges is set, and seccomp is in
filter mode.

267
00:23:40,795 --> 00:23:47,982
The cgroup shows 512 megabytes of memory, no
swap, 128 processes, one CPU.

268
00:23:47,982 --> 00:23:51,433
Of 200 processes started, 80 were refused.

269
00:23:51,433 --> 00:23:56,620
Allocating past the limit returned limit
exceeded, exit 137.

270
00:23:56,620 --> 00:24:00,345
A runaway loop with a three second timeout
returned timed out.

271
00:24:00,801 --> 00:24:18,001
While a sandbox container ran, docker
inspect on the host showed user 1000,
network none, a read-only root, all
capabilities dropped, no-new-privileges,
memory, CPU and process limits, a noexec
tmp, and one read-only mount at work.

272
00:24:18,455 --> 00:24:23,880
Now Lumen Goods, the demo repository,
through the same executor with npm test.

273
00:24:23,880 --> 00:24:27,768
The executor returned status exited, exit
code zero.

274
00:24:27,768 --> 00:24:31,793
Its output reads ten tests, ten pass, zero
fail.

275
00:24:31,792 --> 00:24:35,055
This run makes no claim about the planted
regression.

276
00:24:35,509 --> 00:24:39,559
Dependency preparation was not run here;
this is from the docs.

277
00:24:39,559 --> 00:24:45,921
A disposable container runs npm ci without
scripts on an internal network with no
route.

278
00:24:45,922 --> 00:24:48,822
Only an egress proxy reaches the registry.

279
00:24:48,822 --> 00:24:53,534
Then the network is removed, and the result
is sealed and mounted read-only.

280
00:24:54,003 --> 00:24:56,828
Without Docker, nothing falls back to the
host.

281
00:24:56,828 --> 00:25:01,315
A disabled executor reports unavailable, and
its run call throws.

282
00:25:01,315 --> 00:25:07,066
The pipeline code turns that into a blocked
stage, with the reason sandbox unavailable.


283
00:25:08,020 --> 00:25:10,372
This cart page has one planted bug.

284
00:25:10,672 --> 00:25:16,648
A model plans browser steps, code validates
them, and the sandbox runs them before the
patch.

285
00:25:16,948 --> 00:25:21,100
After the patch the same steps rerun; rules
derive the outcome.

286
00:25:21,400 --> 00:25:28,336
The review page shows both screenshots, and
may claim a visible change only if the
reproduction check passed.

287
00:25:28,636 --> 00:25:32,644
Without Docker, the hosted demo records
these runs as blocked.


288
00:25:33,544 --> 00:25:37,600
A screenshot is stored, then read by a
vision model on its own.

289
00:25:37,900 --> 00:25:42,820
It is checked by its bytes, stored by hash
and queued in the same transaction.

290
00:25:43,120 --> 00:25:50,296
The model sees only the image, never the
report text, and its output keeps visible
and inferred items apart.

291
00:25:50,596 --> 00:25:57,148
Optionally, a segmentation model marks where
an element is, as a display aid, never
evidence.


292
00:25:58,048 --> 00:26:01,840
A recording is analyzed from a few frames,
never the video.

293
00:26:02,140 --> 00:26:08,188
The browser takes up to twelve keyframes and
sends only those; the video stays on the
computer.

294
00:26:08,488 --> 00:26:15,304
The worker reads each frame; a text-only
analysis then sees only the readings and
timestamps.

295
00:26:15,604 --> 00:26:23,932
Recording evidence is labelled reported or
inferred, never observed, so it cannot move
a hypothesis to supported or contradicted.


296
00:26:24,882 --> 00:26:27,157
A screen recording is evidence too.

297
00:26:27,157 --> 00:26:38,644
In the new-investigation form I attach a
real screen recording of the shop-ui cart
bug: someone opens the cart, adds a second
lamp, and sees shipping charged at exactly
fifty dollars.

298
00:26:38,644 --> 00:26:40,332
The form says what will happen.

299
00:26:40,332 --> 00:26:45,219
The browser takes up to twelve still frames
from the file, and uploads only those.

300
00:26:45,219 --> 00:26:47,932
The recording itself never leaves the
computer.

301
00:26:48,392 --> 00:26:51,742
The keyframes are taken in the browser and
sent one by one.

302
00:26:52,198 --> 00:26:56,710
Then a live Muse Spark model reads the
frames, and the wait is sped up.

303
00:26:56,710 --> 00:26:59,260
The panel says analyzed, not watched.

304
00:26:59,724 --> 00:27:03,074
Here are the twelve keyframes, each with its
timestamp.

305
00:27:03,530 --> 00:27:08,067
What is visible, by time: the model
describes the cart at each frame.

306
00:27:08,068 --> 00:27:12,955
One lamp, a subtotal of twenty-five dollars,
shipping four ninety-nine.

307
00:27:12,955 --> 00:27:20,942
Later, two lamps, a subtotal of fifty
dollars, and shipping still four
ninety-nine, while the banner promises free
shipping.

308
00:27:20,942 --> 00:27:22,367
This is a model's reading.

309
00:27:22,367 --> 00:27:26,393
It can contain errors, and it has not been
checked against the repository.

310
00:27:26,848 --> 00:27:37,973
Model inferences are kept apart, for example
that the quantity changed between two
frames, and a candidate list of reproduction
steps that is labelled a guess, not a
verified procedure.

311
00:27:38,430 --> 00:27:42,055
And the panel lists what could not be
determined from the keyframes.

312
00:27:42,524 --> 00:27:47,836
A segmentation model, SAM, marked a region
in one frame, here the banner.

313
00:27:47,836 --> 00:27:52,962
That is a display aid: it shows where a
model points, not proof of what is broken.

314
00:27:53,410 --> 00:27:58,060
The investigation uses this analysis as
reported and inferred context.

315
00:27:58,060 --> 00:28:03,810
It is never treated as observed evidence, so
on its own it cannot make a hypothesis
supported.

316
00:28:03,810 --> 00:28:08,910
I ran this one in analysis-only mode,
because this scene needs no sandbox.


317
00:28:09,832 --> 00:28:15,616
Apply, commit and push are separate
requests; authorizing one never covers
another.

318
00:28:15,916 --> 00:28:19,948
Approve is a signed-in review decision, and
changes no files.

319
00:28:20,248 --> 00:28:23,128
Apply puts the diff into the local working
tree.

320
00:28:23,428 --> 00:28:29,092
Commit records the diff on a new branch
after a secret scan, without touching the
checkout.

321
00:28:29,392 --> 00:28:35,200
Push sends that branch to a named remote,
never forced, and refuses default branches.

322
00:28:35,500 --> 00:28:41,476
All three need an owner session, every
attempt is audited, and refusals use stable
codes.


323
00:28:42,376 --> 00:28:47,008
Nothing is captured until the user clicks,
and everything is previewed first.

324
00:28:47,308 --> 00:28:52,828
First run records consent and a token; a
click captures the tab or a region.

325
00:28:53,128 --> 00:28:58,936
The user blacks out regions, burned into the
pixels, then reviews exactly what is sent.

326
00:28:59,236 --> 00:29:04,684
Upload sends only a bearer token;
idempotency keys make retries safe.

327
00:29:04,984 --> 00:29:11,440
The upload queues a vision read, stored as
derived evidence; an investigation is
optional.

328
00:29:11,740 --> 00:29:16,156
It can only reach the API address; web pages
cannot message it.


329
00:29:17,106 --> 00:29:23,356
This is the Chrome extension, used for real:
a real Chromium with the extension loaded
unpacked.

330
00:29:23,356 --> 00:29:31,294
The page is a static copy of the Lumen Goods
cart page that I wrote for this recording,
because repository code never runs on the
host.

331
00:29:31,294 --> 00:29:40,981
It shows the bug: a subtotal of fifty
dollars, shipping of four ninety-nine, under
a banner that promises free shipping on
orders of fifty dollars or more.

332
00:29:41,432 --> 00:29:42,320
First run.

333
00:29:42,320 --> 00:29:52,194
Before anything else, the extension says
what it collects, and what it never
collects: no cookies, no site storage, no
form values, no other tabs.

334
00:29:52,194 --> 00:29:56,207
Nothing is sent until I review the exact
data and press Send.

335
00:29:56,207 --> 00:29:57,044
I agree.

336
00:29:57,502 --> 00:30:03,489
Then it needs a personal access token,
created in Tracera for the extension client
with four scopes.

337
00:30:03,489 --> 00:30:06,277
It is pasted once, and the field hides it.

338
00:30:06,277 --> 00:30:12,890
Check and save asks the API about the token
before anything is stored, and it stays in
this browser profile.

339
00:30:13,356 --> 00:30:15,269
Nothing has been captured so far.

340
00:30:15,268 --> 00:30:25,319
A script cannot click Chrome's toolbar, so
this recording runs the extension's own
action on the cart tab through Chrome's
debugging protocol, which is the action a
click runs.

341
00:30:25,318 --> 00:30:30,206
The popup opens and names the page, and I
press Capture the visible area.

342
00:30:30,674 --> 00:30:35,099
The screenshot opens in the editor, taken
from the tab by Chrome itself.

343
00:30:35,099 --> 00:30:37,724
I choose Black out and drag over the header.

344
00:30:38,176 --> 00:30:44,938
Blacked-out areas are replaced by black
pixels in the image before upload, so the
server never receives what was hidden.

345
00:30:44,938 --> 00:30:49,026
I give the report a title and a description,
and pick the repository.

346
00:30:49,494 --> 00:30:57,744
Review shows the exact requests that will go
to the API, in order, with the page address
stripped of its query string and fragment.

347
00:30:57,744 --> 00:30:59,144
Nothing else is sent.

348
00:30:59,612 --> 00:31:05,362
Send creates the bug report, uploads the
screenshot as evidence, and starts an
investigation.

349
00:31:05,818 --> 00:31:11,143
In the Tracera web app, that investigation
holds the report and its screenshot
evidence.

350
00:31:11,592 --> 00:31:21,655
After about two minutes, shown here as a
time-lapse, the investigation page holds the
report with its expected and actual values,
and the screenshot attached as evidence.

351
00:31:21,655 --> 00:31:25,905
The black box is the region I blacked out:
it is in the uploaded image.

352
00:31:25,905 --> 00:31:35,167
Beside it is a reading of the screenshot by
a live model, labelled as read from the
pixels, possibly with reading errors, and
not checked against the repository.

353
00:31:35,167 --> 00:31:38,405
It is inferred context, never observed fact.


354
00:31:39,370 --> 00:31:45,010
The MCP server is a thin local client of the
REST API for coding agents.

355
00:31:45,310 --> 00:31:49,870
The agent starts it over standard input and
output; it opens no port.

356
00:31:50,170 --> 00:31:54,898
Calls use a bearer token with rate limits,
and are audited without arguments.

357
00:31:55,198 --> 00:31:58,030
Eleven of its fourteen tools are read-only.

358
00:31:58,330 --> 00:32:03,106
Three create something: a question, a bug
report or an investigation.

359
00:32:03,406 --> 00:32:10,438
It can never approve a patch, apply, commit,
push or run commands, even with all six
scopes.


360
00:32:11,388 --> 00:32:14,225
A coding agent can use Tracera through MCP.

361
00:32:14,226 --> 00:32:22,363
This is the real stdio server, started by a
real MCP client, and every frame in this
scene is captured from that session.

362
00:32:22,363 --> 00:32:23,663
Nothing is staged.

363
00:32:23,663 --> 00:32:25,863
The client lists 14 tools.

364
00:32:25,863 --> 00:32:32,501
Most are read-only, and three create
something: a question, a bug report, or an
investigation.

365
00:32:32,954 --> 00:32:35,491
First call: list the repositories.

366
00:32:35,491 --> 00:32:39,929
The server answers with the registered
repositories and their index status.

367
00:32:40,384 --> 00:32:44,534
Second: ask where free shipping is decided,
and read the answer.

368
00:32:44,534 --> 00:32:49,309
It is labelled live, and 5 of 5 claims are
marked as supported by evidence.

369
00:32:49,309 --> 00:32:51,871
Each claim carries the excerpts it cites.

370
00:32:52,326 --> 00:32:56,801
Then the findings of the investigation from
the Meet scene: one hypothesis.

371
00:32:56,801 --> 00:33:03,189
Its state is confirmed by verification, and
a rule set that state, not the agent and not
the model.

372
00:33:03,644 --> 00:33:09,794
And the patch diff, with what actually ran:
the one-line change, the checks, and the
recorded outcome.

373
00:33:09,794 --> 00:33:13,569
The outcome is verified, and it is read from
the stored record.

374
00:33:14,026 --> 00:33:16,463
MCP clients read and propose.

375
00:33:16,463 --> 00:33:21,788
The tool list has no tool that approves a
patch, applies it, commits, or pushes.

376
00:33:21,788 --> 00:33:26,863
A person approves in the web app, and
applying is a separate, authorized action.

377
00:33:26,863 --> 00:33:31,276
The token used here was scoped to this
client, and it was revoked afterwards.


378
00:33:32,204 --> 00:33:36,836
Tracera's data lives in PostgreSQL, in six
groups of tables.

379
00:33:37,136 --> 00:33:41,480
Identity and access holds users, sessions
and tokens.

380
00:33:41,780 --> 00:33:45,860
Repositories and graph holds snapshots and
the code graph.

381
00:33:46,160 --> 00:33:50,264
Reports and evidence holds bug reports and
evidence assets.

382
00:33:50,564 --> 00:33:57,164
Investigations hold steps, hypotheses,
sandbox runs, patches and verification runs.

383
00:33:57,464 --> 00:34:00,752
Actions and audit hold an append-only audit
log.

384
00:34:01,052 --> 00:34:05,732
Meetings hold transcripts, and confirming a
candidate creates a bug report.

385
00:34:06,032 --> 00:34:13,064
Row level security locks the Data API,
queries are workspace scoped, and evidence
is kept ninety days.


386
00:34:13,964 --> 00:34:19,604
pg-boss is a job queue inside PostgreSQL: no
Redis, no broker.

387
00:34:19,904 --> 00:34:26,312
The API enqueues a job in the same
transaction as its rows, so it exists only
if they commit.

388
00:34:26,612 --> 00:34:32,372
The worker takes one job at a time; a stage
commits its outcome and the next job
together.

389
00:34:32,672 --> 00:34:38,840
A heartbeat reports status every fifteen
seconds; a sweep every minute recovers
stalled steps.


390
00:34:39,740 --> 00:34:46,676
Everything outside the line is untrusted
data; inside it, a model proposes and code
decides.

391
00:34:46,976 --> 00:34:52,688
Zod validates every boundary, and keys and
tokens are redacted before any model call.

392
00:34:52,988 --> 00:34:59,876
Models never act: they return strict JSON,
and code checks citations and sets
hypothesis states.

393
00:35:00,176 --> 00:35:06,416
Nothing runs on the host: tests, installs
and browsers run in locked-down Docker
containers.

394
00:35:06,716 --> 00:35:15,092
Queries are workspace-scoped; apply, commit
and push are separate owner requests that
tokens and MCP can never make.

395
00:35:15,392 --> 00:35:20,336
Further guarantees cover the database,
honest labels and repository paths.


396
00:35:21,236 --> 00:35:25,124
The same code runs in both; the hosted demo
has no Docker.

397
00:35:25,424 --> 00:35:31,256
Hosted, a Vercel site forwards API calls to
one Render service with Render Postgres.

398
00:35:31,556 --> 00:35:39,836
So the hosted demo is analysis only:
reproduction and verification are blocked;
apply, commit and push are refused.

399
00:35:40,136 --> 00:35:47,528
Locally, two commands start Postgres, the
API, the worker and the web app; Docker can
run the sandbox.


400
00:35:48,478 --> 00:35:50,578
Security is part of the design.

401
00:35:50,578 --> 00:35:57,740
Repository code never runs on the host: it
runs in a Docker sandbox with no network and
limited resources.

402
00:35:57,740 --> 00:35:59,128
Models never act.

403
00:35:59,128 --> 00:36:02,565
They return strict structured output that
code validates.

404
00:36:02,565 --> 00:36:10,278
Approval changes no files, and applying,
committing, or pushing are separate actions
that only a signed-in owner can authorize.

405
00:36:10,278 --> 00:36:20,653
Secrets stay on the server and are redacted
before every model call, every query is
scoped to a workspace, and repository text
is treated as untrusted data.

406
00:36:21,108 --> 00:36:22,596
Now the honest limits.

407
00:36:22,596 --> 00:36:29,183
The hosted site has no sandbox, so
investigations there stop after analysis,
and the page says so.

408
00:36:29,183 --> 00:36:37,046
Models can be wrong, which is why every
claim is labelled live or mock, observed or
inferred, and a person decides.

409
00:36:37,046 --> 00:36:44,096
Recording analysis rests on a few sampled
keyframes, and its readings are inferred
context, never observed.

410
00:36:44,096 --> 00:36:49,146
And the laptop needs the internet for model
calls, and for the optional Google Meet
import.

411
00:36:49,610 --> 00:36:53,160
To run it yourself you need Node, git, and
Docker.

412
00:36:53,160 --> 00:37:04,972
Run npm ci, then npm run setup, which checks
the prerequisites, creates the environment
file, starts PostgreSQL, and creates the
owner and the demo repositories.

413
00:37:04,972 --> 00:37:09,697
Then npm run local starts the API, the
worker, and the web app.

414
00:37:09,697 --> 00:37:14,760
Open it at 127.0.0.1, not localhost.

415
00:37:14,760 --> 00:37:19,172
For live models, set the Meta provider and
key in the environment file.

416
00:37:19,624 --> 00:37:20,437
Tracera.

417
00:37:20,437 --> 00:37:22,212
From symptom to certainty.

418
00:37:22,212 --> 00:37:26,287
Evidence-backed, honest about what ran, and
a person decides.

419
00:37:26,287 --> 00:37:27,087
Thank you.

